I shall yow telle of a ''ventur'' certeyn,
And that a strange, if it please yow to here.
{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 15}}
}}
{{smaller block|To desperate ''ventures'' and assured destruction.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., v. 3. 31}}}}

'''2.''' Specifically, a scheme for making gain b way of trade; a commercial speculation.
{{smaller block|<poem>I, in this ''venture'', double gains pursue,
And laid out all my stock to purchase you.</poem>
{{right|''Dryde''}}}}

'''3.''' The thing put to hazard; a stake; a risk particularly, something sent to sea in trade.
{{smaller block|My ''ventures'' are not in one bottom trusted.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|May every merchant here see safe his ''ventures!''
{{right|''Fletcher'', Beggars’ Bush, v.}}}}
{{smaller block|Certainly Aristophanes had no ''Venture'' at Sea, or el must think the Trident signified but very little.
{{right|''J. Collier'', Short View (ed. 1698), p.}}}}

'''4.''' Chance; hap; contingency; luck; an eve that is not or cannot be foreseen.
{{smaller block|Yef thow haddest do alle the gode dedes of the worl and thyn ende were euell, thow were in a ''venture'' all to lese. {{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), i.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Venture'' hath place in love.
{{right|''Earl of Oxford'' (Arber’s Eng. Garner, I. 59}}}}

'''At a venture''', at hazard; without seeing the end mark, or without foreseeing the issue; at random.
{{smaller block|<poem>So fourth she went and left all other thing,
''At a venture'' your welefare for to see.</poem>
{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 12}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|A certain man drew a bow ''at a venture.'' {{right|1 Ki. xxii. 34.}}}}
='''Syn. 1.''' ''Hazard'', etc. See ''risk''<sup>1</sup>.

'''venture''' (ven′t̮ūr), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''ventured'', ppr. ''venturing.'' [By apheresis from ''aventure'', ''adventure'', ''v.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To dare; have courage or presumption, as to do, undertake, or say.
{{smaller block|<poem>To whom alone I ''venture'' to complain.</poem>
{{right|''Congreve'', To a Candle.}}}}
'''2.''' To run a hazard or risk; try the chance; make a venture; expose one’s life, fortune, etc.
{{smaller block|There is also a Rope stretched cross the Street brest high, and no man may pass this place till he is examin’d, unless he will ''venture'' to be soundly bang’d by the Watch.
{{right|''Dampier'', Voyages, II. i. 77.}}
''Shal.'' Break their talk, Mistress Quickly; my kinsman shall speak for himself.
''Slen.'' I’ll make a shaft or a bolt on ’t: ’slid, ’tis but ''venturing.'' {{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., iii. 4. 25.}}
<poem>Let him ''venture''
In some decay’d crare of his own.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Captain, i. 2.}}
You have greatly ''ventured;'' but all must do so who would greatly win. {{right|''Byron.''}}}}
'''To venture at, to venture on''' or '''upon''', {{smaller|to dare to engage in; attempt without any certainty of success.}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To expose to hazard; risk; stake.
{{smaller block|<poem>We all are soldiers, and all ''venture'' lives.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', King and No King, i. 1.}}
If every hair of my head were a man, in this quarrel I would ''venture'' them all.
{{right|Quoted in ''Macaulay’s'' Hist. Eng., v.}}}}
'''2.''' To run the hazard of; expose one’s self to.
{{smaller block|<poem>I should ''venture'' purgatory for ’t.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, iv. 3. 77.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|No, no, I’ll walk late no more; I ought less to ''venture'' it than other people, and so I was told.
{{right|''Swift'', Journal to Stella, June 30, 1711.}}}}
'''3.''' To put or send on a venture or commercial speculation.
{{smaller block|The catle were y<sup>e</sup> best goods, for y<sup>e</sup> other, being ''ventured'' ware, were neither at y<sup>e</sup> best (some of them) nor at y<sup>e</sup> best prises.
{{right|''Bradford'', Plymouth Plantation, p. 201.}}}}
'''4.''' To confide in; rely on; trust. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|A man would be well enough pleased to buy silks of one whom he would not ''venture'' to feel his pulse.
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 21.}}}}

'''venturer''' (ven′t̮ūr-ėr), ''n.'' [< ''venture'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' One who ventures or adventures; one who risks life, property, etc.; one who causes risk; one who puts to hazard.
{{smaller block|A merchant ''venturer'' of daintie meate.
{{right|''Nashe'', Pierce Penilesse, p. 48.}}
The ''venturers'' with the sword were sixty thousand in number, . . . because Mustafa had dispersed a rumour . . . that Famagusta was much more wealthy and rich then the citie of Nicosia was.
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', II. i. 129.}}}}
'''2†.''' A prostitute; a strumpet. ''Webster''.—'''Merchant Venturers†.''' {{smaller|Same as ''Merchant Adventurers''. See ''adventurer''.}}

'''venturesome''' (ven′t̮ūr-sum), ''a.'' [< ''venture'' + ''-some''. Cf. ''adventuresome''.] Inclined to venture; venturous; bold; daring; adventurous; intrepid; hazardous.
{{smaller block|That bold and ''venturesome'' act of his.
{{right|''Strype'', Eccles. Mem., Henry VIII., an. 1546.}}
But for the chance preservation of the word in Latin, it might seem ''venturesome'' to make Spanish explain Umbrian.
{{right|''Amer. Jour. Philol.'', VI. 244.}}}}
